---
/**
 * The site root.
 *
 * Deliberately not the chapter list: that is `/learn/`, and duplicating it
 * here would leave two pages competing for the same searches with the same
 * words. This page says what the site is and what the promise costs to keep,
 * and hands off. It is also the page that has room to grow when something on
 * this domain is not a chapter.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { navTracks, guideHref, pageHref } from "../nav";
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION } from "../seo";
import { CHANNELS, LABELS, channelUrl, shorts } from "../channels";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const tracks = await navTracks();
const chapterCount = tracks.reduce(
  (n, track) =>
    n +
    track.sections.reduce(
      (m, section) => m + section.groups.reduce((k, group) => k + group.members.length, 0),
      0,
    ),
  0,
);

// The newest few. Text and a link until the reader presses Play, so the home
// page loads nothing from YouTube on its own (src/scripts/riddle.ts).
const riddles = (await shorts()).slice(0, 4);
---

<Base
  title="Zig Guide Live"
  seoTitle="Zig Tutorial: Every Snippet Verified Against Master"
  description={SITE_DESCRIPTION}
>
  <h1>Zig Guide Live</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A tutorial for the Zig programming language. <strong>Each build compiles
    and runs every snippet against current Zig master</strong>. If an example on
    this site were out of date, the build would fail.
  </p>
  <p>
    The snippets run in your browser as WebAssembly. Your browser runs the same
    artifact that CI verified. There is no server, and you install nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Zig changes fast. If people only proofread documentation, it becomes out of
    date, and nobody sees the problem. Every night, CI verifies this guide again
    against a new compiler. The site publishes again only when every snippet
    still passes. For the exact mechanism, read{" "}
    <a href={`${base}verification/`}>how this guide is verified</a>. That page
    also tells you what occurs to the snippets that a browser cannot run.
    If you use a tagged release, read{" "}
    <a href={pageHref("getting-started/coming-from-older-zig")}>what changed</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href={guideHref}>Read the guide →</a>
    {" or "}
    <a href={`${base}paths/`}>select a reading path</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What is in it</h2>
  <p>
    The guide has {chapterCount} chapters in seven tracks, in one reading order.
    The arrows at the bottom of every page follow that order. Thus you can read
    the guide from the start to the end.
  </p>
  {/* A track has no page of its own, on purpose, so each one links to where it
      begins. That is the same place the sidebar and the pager would take you. */}
  <ul class="section-index">
    {
      tracks.map((track) => (
        <li>
          <a href={pageHref(track.sections[0].slug)}>{track.title}</a>
          <span>{track.blurb}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p><a href={guideHref}>Every section, in order →</a></p>

  {
    riddles.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2>Riddle breaks</h2>
        <p>
          Short videos from the author of this guide. Most are riddles: each
          riddle asks the question first and shows the answer at the end. Some
          are jokes. The videos play here, on this page.
        </p>
        <ul class="riddle-strip">
          {riddles.map((short) => (
            <li data-short-card>
              <p class="riddle-question">{short.title}</p>
              <a
                class="riddle-play"
                href={short.url}
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                data-short={short.id}
                data-title={short.title}
              >
                {LABELS[short.kind].play}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p>
          {CHANNELS.map((channel, i) => (
            <>
              {i > 0 && " · "}
              <a href={channelUrl(channel)} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                More from {channel.name} on YouTube →
              </a>
            </>
          ))}
        </p>
      </>
    )
  }

  {/* The Run button is the promise this site makes, so the floor it needs is
      worth stating once, on the page a reader lands on. The browser versions
      are the build target the scripts are compiled to, not a guess. */}
  <h2>What your browser needs</h2>
  <p>
    To run a snippet, your browser needs WebAssembly and JavaScript, and nothing
    else. The scripts here are built for Chrome and Edge 111, Firefox 114, and
    Safari 16.4 (iOS and iPadOS 16.4) or newer. On an older browser, or with
    JavaScript off, you can still read every chapter. Every snippet is on the
    page, highlighted and complete. Only the Run button is missing.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      When you press Run, the page downloads one <code>.wasm</code> file. The
      file is about 70 KB for a typical snippet and about 150 KB for the largest.
      The snippets that show a safety check fail are an exception, at about
      1.2 MB each. The page keeps the compiled module, so a second run of the
      same snippet downloads nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      The four chapters that teach vectors are compiled with WebAssembly SIMD.
      Without SIMD, the compiler would remove the vector code, with no warning.
      Firefox disables wasm SIMD on x86 processors without SSE4.1, in all
      versions. Thus those four chapters can fail to load on a machine where
      every other snippet runs. If that occurs, the playground names the
      missing requirement, and does not show the raw engine error.
    </li>
    <li>
      When you first edit a snippet, the page loads a code editor of about
      500 KB. The page does not load the editor when you only read or run.
    </li>
    <li>
      You install nothing, and nothing runs on a server. A snippet runs in the
      sandbox of the page. The sandbox has only an empty directory in memory,
      and no network and no input. Thus some examples say that CI compiled them,
      but that they cannot run here.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who writes it</h2>
  <p>
    Rajesh Pillai writes this guide, with AI assistance. It is an unofficial
    community guide, and is not affiliated with the Zig Software Foundation.
    The documentation of the foundation is at{" "}
    <a href="https://ziglang.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >ziglang.org</a>. Read more about <a href={`${base}about/`}>what this guide
    is and what it is not</a>, and how to <a href={`${base}contact/`}>report an
    error in a chapter</a>.
  </p>
</Base>

<script>
  import "../scripts/riddle.ts";
</script>
